.TH MOUNT.FILSYS 1 "September 6, 2026" "filsys 1.8.0" "File Format and Disk Utilities"
.SH NAME
mount.filsys, umount.filsys \- mount and dismount a Research Unix filesystem
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount.filsys
.BI \-v " edition"
.RB [ \-o
.IR offset= N [, version= N ][, arch= NAME ][, uid= N , gid= N ,...]]
.RB [ \-r ]
.RB [ \-f ]
.RB [ \-d ]
.RB [ \-F ]
.I image mountpoint
.br
.B mount.filsys
.BI \-v " edition"
.RB [ \-o
.IR offset= N ]
.BI \-c " image"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mount.filsys
announces to the system (via FUSE) that a removable Research Unix filesystem
is present at
.IR image .
The file
.I mountpoint
must exist already; it must be a directory (unless the root of the mounted
filesystem is not a directory).  It becomes the name of the newly mounted
root.
.PP
A Research Unix disk image holds one or more filesystems
.RI ( partitions ),
selected by a partition table that is compiled into the kernel, not stored on
the disk.  By default the filesystem at block 0 is mounted; the
.B offset=
option mounts a filesystem that begins at a byte offset elsewhere in the file.
.PP
The on-disk edition is selected at run time with
.BR \-v :
pdp7 selects the word-addressed PDP-7 filesystem; v1, v2, or v3 select the First
through Third Edition format (one on-disk layout); v4, v5, or v6 select the
Sixth Edition format (V4 and V5 are byte-identical to V6 on disk); v7 selects
Seventh Edition; vax32 selects 32V (V7 recompiled for the VAX, whose multi-byte
fields are little-endian rather than PDP-11 middle-endian); coherent selects
the Mark Williams Coherent format (V7 with a 64-entry free cache, an
.I s_unique
field, and an
.I s_m/s_n
cylinder interleave; byte order is PDP-11 middle-endian like V7); xenix selects
the SCO Xenix format (little-endian, 1024-byte blocks, a 100-entry free cache,
and a magic checked at open); bsd29 and bsd211 select 2.9BSD and 2.11BSD
(1024-byte blocks; bsd29 keeps V7's 24-bit addresses, bsd211 widens them to
32 bits and uses variable-length directory entries); sysiii selects System III
(V7's on-disk layout under its own name); sysvr2 and sysvr4 select System V
Release 2/3 and Release 4 (the one 4-byte-aligned s5fs layout, plus a magic and
a block-size field; R4 additionally stores an s_state clean/dirty word, whose
byte order is set with
.BR arch= ).
A bare number
.RB ( 0 ,
.BR 1 ,
.BR 2 ,
\&...) is also accepted.
.PP
The second form
.RB ( \-c )
does not mount; it walks the free list and the inode table and reports any
integrity problems on standard output, exiting non-zero if any are found.
.PP
The mounted filesystem supports the full set of directory operations: creating
and removing files and directories, renaming (with the
.B ..
and link-count bookkeeping a directory move requires), hard links, and
.I mknod
for device files (see
.BR filsys (5)
for the device-number encoding).  V7 permits the superuser to hard-link a
directory; the driver honours that, though a Linux host's kernel refuses
directory links before FUSE sees them.
.PP
.B mount.filsys
is installed as
.BR sbin/mount.filsys ,
so
.B mount -t filsys
works: mount(8) calls
.B mount.filsys
.I device dir
.RB [ \-o
.IR opts ],
with the edition in
.B version=
and the partition in
.BR offset= .
.PP
.I Umount.filsys
is the historical counterpart: it announced to the system that a mounted
filesystem was to be removed.  With FUSE, unmounting is done with
.BR fusermount3 (1)
.BR \-u ,
so no separate
.B umount.filsys
binary ships; the name is retained here as an alias for the command it
supersedes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-v " edition"
Filesystem edition: one of pdp7, v1, v2, v3, v4, v5, v6, v7, v8, v9, v10,
vax32, coherent, xenix, bsd29, bsd211, sysiii, sysvr2, or sysvr4; or
.B unix
to autodetect.  Optional: omitting
.B \-v
is the same as
.BR "\-v unix" .
.BR "\-v v10"
probes only V10's three on-disk forms (1 KB free list, 4 KB bitmap, 4 KB
bigmap) and errors if none matches.
.TP
.BI \-o " offset= N [, version= N ][, uid= N , gid= N ,...]"
Comma-separated options.  The filsys-specific
.B offset=
is the byte offset of the filesystem within the image (a partition);
.B version=
selects the edition (same values as
.BR \-v );
.B uid=
and
.B gid=
override the reported ownership;
.B packing=
selects the PDP-7 word container codec (rb09, packed18 or rim) for a dump
that is not in simh's 4-byte-slot convention.  Any other option (for example
.BR allow_other )
is passed through to FUSE.  Without
.BR offset= ,
the filesystem at block 0 is mounted.
.TP
.BI \-o " arch= NAME"
Override the byte order the format stores its multi-byte fields in.
The V7-family editions fix their own byte order (V7 is PDP-11 middle-endian,
vax32 and xenix little-endian, coherent middle-endian), so
.B arch=
matters for the System V editions
.RB ( sysiii ,
.BR sysvr2 ,
.BR sysvr4 ),
which ran on many CPUs and left byte order to the architecture.
Recognised names, by byte order: little-endian
.BR vax ,
.BR x86 ,
.BR 386 ,
.BR i386 ,
.BR ns32k ,
.BR ns32000 ,
.BR i860 ,
and
.BR clipper ;
big-endian
.BR 3b2 ,
.BR 3b20 ,
.BR we32000 ,
.BR 68k ,
.BR m68k ,
.BR 68000 ,
.BR sparc ,
.BR mips ,
.BR parisc ,
.BR hppa ,
.BR powerpc ,
and
.BR ppc ;
and PDP-11 middle-endian
.BR pdp11 .
For the editions that carry a superblock magic word
.RB ( xenix ,
.BR sysvr2 ,
.BR sysvr4 ),
the byte order is detected from that magic, so a big-endian System V volume
mounts correctly even when
.B arch=
is omitted; an explicit
.B arch=
that contradicts the magic is refused.
.TP
.B \-r
Mount read-only.
.TP
.B \-f
Stay in the foreground (do not daemonize).
.TP
.B \-F
Open the filesystem even when its superblock magic word matches neither byte
order (a foreign or damaged image).  Only meaningful with
.BR \-o " arch=" ,
which then fixes the byte order to use.
.TP
.B \-d
Enable FUSE debug output (implies
.BR \-f ).
.TP
.B \-c
Run the integrity check instead of mounting (see
.BR fsck.filsys (1)).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
mount.filsys -v v7 rp06-0.disk /mnt/v7
mount.filsys -v v7 -o offset=9416704 rp06-0.disk /mnt/usr
mount.filsys -v v6 v6root.dsk /mnt/v6        # V4/V5/V6 (identical on disk)
mount.filsys -v vax32 32v-root.disk /mnt/32v
mount.filsys -v v7 -c rp06-0.disk            # check instead of mounting
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR findfs.filsys (1),
.BR mkfs.filsys (1),
.BR fsck.filsys (1),
.BR filsys (5),
.BR fusermount3 (1)
